\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {113}{\ignorespaces Optimizer-repeat gate and exclusion audit. The upper-left panel compares zero-signal means from the raw first attempts with the accepted LML-selected states; the visibly repaired $1\%\times 100$, 65\nobreakspace  {}MeV point is numerical rather than a pull-based deletion. The upper-right panel reports accepted factor-15 contacts. The lower-left panel gives the adaptive attempt workload, and the lower-right panel lists all six excluded states. Statistical extremes that pass the pull-blind technical gate remain in the headline summaries; repeat-triggered boundary solutions are retained when replicated.\relax }}{182}{figure.caption.159}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-optimizer-gate}{{113}{182}{Optimizer-repeat gate and exclusion audit. The upper-left panel compares zero-signal means from the raw first attempts with the accepted LML-selected states; the visibly repaired $1\%\times 100$, 65~MeV point is numerical rather than a pull-based deletion. The upper-right panel reports accepted factor-15 contacts. The lower-left panel gives the adaptive attempt workload, and the lower-right panel lists all six excluded states. Statistical extremes that pass the pull-blind technical gate remain in the headline summaries; repeat-triggered boundary solutions are retained when replicated.\relax }{figure.caption.159}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {114}{\ignorespaces Full-100 spurious-signal study. Each row corresponds to one ensemble. The left panels show every accepted zero-signal pull and its median; the middle panels show raw first-attempt means, accepted means with 95\% Student-$t$ intervals, and the deterministic analytic-mean closure; the right panels show accepted sample widths with 95\% normal-theory intervals. Red crosses mark excluded raw fit states where present. No accepted pull is clipped. The common mass dependence between analytic-mean and ensemble results diagnoses stress-truth/model closure rather than a statistical outlier.\relax }}{183}{figure.caption.160}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-zero-signal-spurious}{{114}{183}{Full-100 spurious-signal study. Each row corresponds to one ensemble. The left panels show every accepted zero-signal pull and its median; the middle panels show raw first-attempt means, accepted means with 95\% Student-$t$ intervals, and the deterministic analytic-mean closure; the right panels show accepted sample widths with 95\% normal-theory intervals. Red crosses mark excluded raw fit states where present. No accepted pull is clipped. The common mass dependence between analytic-mean and ensemble results diagnoses stress-truth/model closure rather than a statistical outlier.\relax }{figure.caption.160}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {115}{\ignorespaces Investigation of the $1\%\times 10$, 65\nobreakspace  {}MeV closure offset. The top-left panel separates the pilot and independent reserve distributions; the top-right tracks the running mean and its 95\% Student-$t$ interval, with the pilot/reserve boundary fixed at toy 10. The lower-left normal Q--Q diagnostic shows no isolated tail capable of explaining the mean, and the lower-right panel shows the correlated response across injection strengths. The analytic-mean pull of 0.837 was obtained from the exact stress-truth mean with independent optimizer seeds.\relax }}{184}{figure.caption.161}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-bias-confirmation}{{115}{184}{Investigation of the $1\%\times 10$, 65~MeV closure offset. The top-left panel separates the pilot and independent reserve distributions; the top-right tracks the running mean and its 95\% Student-$t$ interval, with the pilot/reserve boundary fixed at toy 10. The lower-left normal Q--Q diagnostic shows no isolated tail capable of explaining the mean, and the lower-right panel shows the correlated response across injection strengths. The analytic-mean pull of 0.837 was obtained from the exact stress-truth mean with independent optimizer seeds.\relax }{figure.caption.161}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {116}{\ignorespaces Full-100 refmatched closure for $1\%\times 10$. Every cell retains 100 accepted toys. The persistent positive 65\nobreakspace  {}MeV pull means and the corresponding analytic-mean result are local stress-truth/model offsets; the other masses have near-unit widths and substantially smaller means. Crosses in the mean panel show raw first-attempt moments before the predeclared LML/reproducibility selection.\relax }}{185}{figure.caption.162}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-closure-1pct-x10}{{116}{185}{Full-100 refmatched closure for $1\%\times 10$. Every cell retains 100 accepted toys. The persistent positive 65~MeV pull means and the corresponding analytic-mean result are local stress-truth/model offsets; the other masses have near-unit widths and substantially smaller means. Crosses in the mean panel show raw first-attempt moments before the predeclared LML/reproducibility selection.\relax }{figure.caption.162}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {117}{\ignorespaces As in Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:v501-v4p6-closure-1pct-x10}, for the native-10\% ensemble. All cells retain 100 toys. The largest mean offset is at 65\nobreakspace  {}MeV. The only accepted width outside 0.8--1.2 in the complete study is 1.237 for the 65\nobreakspace  {}MeV, $z=5$ cell; its normal-theory 95\% interval is $[1.086,1.437]$.\relax }}{186}{figure.caption.163}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-closure-10pct}{{117}{186}{As in Fig.~\ref {fig:v501-v4p6-closure-1pct-x10}, for the native-10\% ensemble. All cells retain 100 toys. The largest mean offset is at 65~MeV. The only accepted width outside 0.8--1.2 in the complete study is 1.237 for the 65~MeV, $z=5$ cell; its normal-theory 95\% interval is $[1.086,1.437]$.\relax }{figure.caption.163}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {118}{\ignorespaces As in Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:v501-v4p6-closure-1pct-x10}, for $1\%\times 100$. The 90\nobreakspace  {}MeV, $z=3$ cell retains 99 toys after one irreproducible injected refit is excluded; all other cells retain 100. Eighty-five of 1999 accepted fits contact the factor-15 upper length-scale bound. Stable contacts are reported rather than removed or used to change the frozen card.\relax }}{187}{figure.caption.164}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-closure-1pct-x100}{{118}{187}{As in Fig.~\ref {fig:v501-v4p6-closure-1pct-x10}, for $1\%\times 100$. The 90~MeV, $z=3$ cell retains 99 toys after one irreproducible injected refit is excluded; all other cells retain 100. Eighty-five of 1999 accepted fits contact the factor-15 upper length-scale bound. Stable contacts are reported rather than removed or used to change the frozen card.\relax }{figure.caption.164}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {119}{\ignorespaces As in Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:v501-v4p6-closure-1pct-x10}, for $10\%\times 10$. The four 90\nobreakspace  {}MeV cells and the 180\nobreakspace  {}MeV, $z=5$ cell retain 99 toys; all others retain 100. The repeat gate repairs the dramatic 65 and 180\nobreakspace  {}MeV optimizer branches seen in the v4.5 pilot without discarding those background pseudoexperiments. Among 1995 accepted fits, 134 stably contact the factor-15 upper bound.\relax }}{188}{figure.caption.165}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-closure-10pct-x10}{{119}{188}{As in Fig.~\ref {fig:v501-v4p6-closure-1pct-x10}, for $10\%\times 10$. The four 90~MeV cells and the 180~MeV, $z=5$ cell retain 99 toys; all others retain 100. The repeat gate repairs the dramatic 65 and 180~MeV optimizer branches seen in the v4.5 pilot without discarding those background pseudoexperiments. Among 1995 accepted fits, 134 stably contact the factor-15 upper bound.\relax }{figure.caption.165}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Full-100 accepted closure summary over the five requested masses. The maximum mean entries give the mass, and for nonzero injections also $z$, at which the maximum absolute mean occurs. Width ranges span five zero-signal or 15 nonzero cells. Boundary contacts are accepted, stable fit states, not exclusions.\relax }}{189}{table.caption.166}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:v501-v4p6-refmatched-summary}{{25}{189}{Full-100 accepted closure summary over the five requested masses. The maximum mean entries give the mass, and for nonzero injections also $z$, at which the maximum absolute mean occurs. Width ranges span five zero-signal or 15 nonzero cells. Boundary contacts are accepted, stable fit states, not exclusions.\relax }{table.caption.166}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {120}{\ignorespaces Signed $\varepsilon ^2$ extraction-coordinate distributions for the 1-, 3-, and 5-$\sigma _A$ injections. Points are all accepted fits; solid summaries are medians and empirical 16--84\% intervals, while dashed summaries describe the toy-local injected coordinates. Panel-wise symmetric-log axes retain negative fitted values and broad 1-$\sigma $ fluctuations. Because both $K_{stm}$ and $\sigma ^{\mathrm  {ref}}_{A,stm}$ are toy-local, the injected coordinates are distributions. These are extraction diagnostics, not limits, physical negative couplings, or expected bands.\relax }}{190}{figure.caption.167}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-eps2-distributions}{{120}{190}{Signed $\eps ^2$ extraction-coordinate distributions for the 1-, 3-, and 5-$\sigma _A$ injections. Points are all accepted fits; solid summaries are medians and empirical 16--84\% intervals, while dashed summaries describe the toy-local injected coordinates. Panel-wise symmetric-log axes retain negative fitted values and broad 1-$\sigma $ fluctuations. Because both $K_{stm}$ and $\sigma ^{\mathrm {ref}}_{A,stm}$ are toy-local, the injected coordinates are distributions. These are extraction diagnostics, not limits, physical negative couplings, or expected bands.\relax }{figure.caption.167}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {121}{\ignorespaces Direct full-100 comparisons between native 10\% and $1\%\times 10$ (top) and between $10\%\times 10$ and $1\%\times 100$ (bottom). Solid curves and bands are fitted medians and empirical 16--84\% spreads; dashed curves are injected medians. The source functions are fitted independently and the cross-source ensembles are unpaired. Their expected-normalization ratio is 1.1296466 in both comparisons, so differences combine source/selection shape and normalization and are not pure exposure effects or paired tests. The ribbons are descriptive coordinate spreads, not expected-limit bands.\relax }}{191}{figure.caption.168}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:v501-v4p6-direct-eps2-comparisons}{{121}{191}{Direct full-100 comparisons between native 10\% and $1\%\times 10$ (top) and between $10\%\times 10$ and $1\%\times 100$ (bottom). Solid curves and bands are fitted medians and empirical 16--84\% spreads; dashed curves are injected medians. The source functions are fitted independently and the cross-source ensembles are unpaired. Their expected-normalization ratio is 1.1296466 in both comparisons, so differences combine source/selection shape and normalization and are not pure exposure effects or paired tests. The ribbons are descriptive coordinate spreads, not expected-limit bands.\relax }{figure.caption.168}{}}
